{
 "summary": "Adds comprehensive unit test coverage for the `Sock` socket wrapper and OS-level socket readiness invariants across supported platforms. It tests `Sock::Wait()` and `Sock::WaitMany()` readiness reporting matrices, multi-socket event attribution, timeout bounds, listening socket event signaling, non-blocking `connect()` outcomes, level-triggered event semantics, and socket option inheritance on accepted sockets.",
 "problem": "Bitcoin Core's network layers rely on specific platform socket semantics and assumptions (such as `select()` vs `poll()` backend differences and socket option inheritance) that were not thoroughly verified in unit tests. Subtleties like unrequested event reporting or unexpected readiness signals can lead to critical bugs such as CVE-2026-117350.",
 "evidence": [
  "\"This improves our `Sock` test coverage following CVE-2026-117350.\" (PR description)",
  "\"A few notable invariants exercised here: Only what's requested is reported (in particular, no unrequested RecvEvent when we took care not to ask for it, see CVE-2026-117350).\" (Commit f35f2d576a)",
  "\"Check that Recv() and Send() with MSG_DONTWAIT return would-block rather than wait on a socket as Accept() returns it. NOTE: since accepted sockets on Linux do not inherit NONBLOCK, this is the only thing protecting Linux users from a trivial remote forever-stall.\" (Code comment in `src/test/sock_tests.cpp`)"
